WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.280
OpenAI is losing more than two&nbsp;
dollars for every dollar it makes.&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.880
Their Q1 2026 operating margin was -122%.&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.760
They are on pace to undershoot its&nbsp;
ad revenue projections by 90 percent.&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.840
ChatGPT's market share has dropped&nbsp;
below 50% for the first time ever.&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.200
Their enterprise share has&nbsp;
been cut nearly in half.&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.760
And Sam Altman just admitted it himself:&nbsp;
OpenAI has "not had our best 12 months ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.920
and it's mostly my fault."
How did this happen?&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:41.040
ChatGPT has over 900 million weekly users.
It’s the company that started the AI race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:51.760
which is now falling behind.
Can they still catch up?&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:58.080
To answer these questions, we need to look at&nbsp;
the state of OpenAI, now, and how we got here.&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.880
The first tipping point wasn’t&nbsp;
actually Anthropic… but Gemini.&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:09.360
In November 2025, Gemini 3 beat GPT-5.1&nbsp;
across essentially every benchmark.&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.360
This caused Altman to issue&nbsp;
a “code red” at OpenAI.&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:20.720
Though, OpenAI still had a gigantic lead.&nbsp;
Over 1.1 billion users, with Gemini at 662&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:26.880
million, and Claude with 245 million.
But, these numbers are quite deceptive.&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.200
OpenAI has a lot of users… but their control&nbsp;
on the markets that matter, is shrinking.&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.240
In May, ChatGPT’s global assistant share&nbsp;
dropped below 50% for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:47.760
to 46%, with Gemini at 28% and Claude at 10%.
It seems like OpenAI is still in the lead, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.560
things get a lot worse for OpenAI, when we look&nbsp;
at what actually makes money: enterprise spending.&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.800
And oh boy, is it bad.
“We estimate Anthropic now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:07.200
earns 40% of enterprise LLM spend, up from 24%&nbsp;
last year and 12% in 2023. Over the same period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:14.480
OpenAI lost nearly half of its enterprise&nbsp;
share, falling to 27% from 50% in 2023.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:22.560
Google also saw significant gains, increasing its&nbsp;
enterprise share from 7% in 2023 to 21% in 2025.”&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.600
This is one of the biggest foundational problems,&nbsp;
and a big part of this is coding, where LLMs have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.520
seen some of the most successful return.
Anthropic now has 54% of the coding market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.600
with OpenAI at just 21%.
And part of the challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:46.080
is the delay or restraint of computing power.
About 40% of US data centres planned for 2026&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.720
are already delayed, transformer lead times&nbsp;
have stretched to five years in some regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.080
and OpenAI has cancelled sites in&nbsp;
the UK, Norway and Lordstown, Ohio.&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.800
OpenAI is struggling in the deep,&nbsp;
high revenue niches that matter.&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.400
But, there’s another part&nbsp;
we need to talk about. Ads.&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.880
OpenAI, and others, have claimed that ads are&nbsp;
their saving grace. And it very well could be.&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.480
The way to monetise the free tier.
Because, boy are there a lot of them.&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.640
Out of 900-910 million weekly active&nbsp;
users, 95% of them are on the free service.&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.520
Hence why OpenAI set a target of&nbsp;
$100 billion in ad revenue for 2030.&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:33.760
If they can’t dominate coding or enterprise,&nbsp;
maybe they can be the mass market ad service?&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.280
Well…
OpenAI’s ad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:41.200
business is on pace to miss this by 90%.
In fact, the standalone AI chatbots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:47.200
like ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, Google AI Mode,&nbsp;
and more, will have cumulatively generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:53.440
under $1 billion in 2026 in ad revenue.
By 2030? It’s expected to be just $5.41&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.480
billion, across the entire market.
“OpenAI's single-company 2030 target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:04.720
is roughly 20 times larger than eMarketer's&nbsp;
estimate for the entire U.S. chatbot ad market.”&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.320
But, why?
They have so many users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.320
why can’t they monetise them?
A few reasons.&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.480
It turns out, prompt replies are&nbsp;
surprisingly hard to advertise on.&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:22.480
Only about 2% of ChatGPT prompts, aka what&nbsp;
the person is searching, involve purchasable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:28.080
products. So it’s hard to sell something,&nbsp;
when the prompt doesn’t relate in any way.&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:33.680
Hence, ChatGPT ads have a terrible&nbsp;
click through rate of 0.91-1.3%.&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.400
For context, Google search ads are around&nbsp;
29.2%. Where basically everything you search,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.160
has some kind of relevant ad.
AI might be taking a lot of web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:49.440
traffic from search results… but it sure&nbsp;
doesn’t seem to be taking the ad revenue.&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.640
According to advertisers, the ad&nbsp;
tech stack is also still “primitive”.&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:00.720
“CPMs on the ad business have already&nbsp;
collapsed from $60 to as low as $25”&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:07.680
To make matters worse, 80% of users sent fewer&nbsp;
than 1,000 messages all year, in 2025. An average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:15.280
of fewer than 3 prompts per day. So the chance&nbsp;
to see ads, let alone relevant ads, isn’t great.&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:21.520
But with all that said, none of that&nbsp;
matters if OpenAI is actually making money.&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.920
So, how is that looking?
Well, OpenAI have two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.760
gigantic problems to deal&nbsp;
with before they can start.&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.960
Let’s look at things right now:
OpenAI has forecasted a $14 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:47.760
loss for all of 2026, with losses totalling&nbsp;
$44 billion from 2023 through to 2029.&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:56.320
But then, we got the Q1 2026 results, with a $6.95&nbsp;
billion operating loss on $5.7 billion in revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:02.400
which is a -122% operating margin.
So, for every dollar OpenAI made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.600
they spent more than two.
They are one quarter into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.320
financial year and have already used half&nbsp;
of the loss they projected for all of it.&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:17.600
Once you add stock compensation, $2.3 billion, the&nbsp;
GAAP operating loss is as high as $9.3 billion.&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.400
FutureSearch now forecasts a&nbsp;
$33 billion GAAP loss for 2026.&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.120
But as we’ll soon find out, these&nbsp;
losses aren’t nearly the entire picture.&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.240
OpenAI needs to start making some real&nbsp;
money, which is proving difficult.&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:36.160
OpenAI promised revenue would&nbsp;
start booming… eventually.&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.720
And revenue was… and technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.360
still is growing, but…
Not so much anymore.&nbsp;

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.920
[Dan Schwarz, FutureSearch]:“OpenAI grew&nbsp;
from $6 billion to roughly $25 billion ARR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:54.000
over the 17 months to May 2026. Growth then&nbsp;
flattened: the run rate held near $25 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.000
from February through the spring. OpenAI's&nbsp;
internal target for mid-2027 is $62 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.800
My forecast median is $44 billion.”
ChatGPT weekly active users have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.080
also plateaued at around 900 million, with&nbsp;
only 50 million being paying subscribers.&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.160
OpenAI can’t seem to monetise&nbsp;
their exorbitant user-base.&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:19.200
Which, is important, because of OpenAI’s&nbsp;
massive bills and capital commitments.&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.640
Starting in 2027, OpenAI will be paying&nbsp;
Oracle $300 billion across 5 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:31.440
for cloud capacity, about 4.5 GW, or the&nbsp;
electricity of about four million homes.&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:37.440
They have a deal with AWS for $38 billion across&nbsp;
seven years, for access to mass amounts of Nvidia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:44.960
GB200 and GB300 GPUs. Then it expanded that&nbsp;
agreement by $100 billion across eight years.&nbsp;

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:50.800
OpenAI has also committed to $250 billion&nbsp;
in Microsoft Azure, aka cloud services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:57.040
as well as 10 GW of custom AI accelerators from&nbsp;
Broadcom. Basically, OpenAI will design its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:02.400
chip, with Broadcom building it. We don’t know&nbsp;
exactly how much this will be, but the Financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:09.040
Times estimated between $350 and $500 billion.
There are even more agreements, but in total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:17.120
according to CFO Sarah Friar, OpenAI has current&nbsp;
commitments of $750 billion through 2030. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:24.000
remember, there’s way more spend beyond 2030…
Though, we also need extra context.&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:30.480
These are massive bills, but most of these&nbsp;
companies are also funneling money into OpenAI.&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:36.480
They just announced a $110 billion funding round,&nbsp;
twice the size of their last raise. “Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:41.920
invested $50 billion, Nvidia invested $30 billion&nbsp;
and SoftBank invested $30 billion in the round”.&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:49.600
Worldwide AI spending is estimated to reach&nbsp;
$2.59 trillion in 2026, a 47% increase over 2025.&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:55.040
The Magnificent Seven have planned more&nbsp;
than $700 billion in AI capex for 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:00.880
up from roughly $400 billion in 2025.
This gives us insight into OpenAI’s runway.&nbsp;

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:07.440
Their cash reserves and marketable securities&nbsp;
are at $73 billion. So they do have cash on hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:13.600
and that’s up from $40 billion at the end of 2025.
But, they’ve promised a lot of money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.080
to quite a few companies.
And they’re burning through cash very quickly.&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.920
So, can OpenAI catch up?
One thing people point to for this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:26.960
is their IPO. This has potential&nbsp;
for a gigantic capital injection.&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.560
Sam Altman believes OpenAI has a&nbsp;
valuation well over $1 trillion.&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.720
But, now they’ve pushed it back, to next year.
And, it makes sense as to why.&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:41.760
OpenAI isn’t winning the race anymore.
Well, they are in pure user counts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.000
but not much else.
Which brings us to the big problem.&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:51.440
OpenAI signed contractual obligations&nbsp;
against revenue that hadn’t arrived yet.&nbsp;

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:58.000
But OpenAI was winning. So they committed big.
Though now, they’re no longer winning the AI race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.280
but still spent the winnings of said race…
Here’s where things get really interesting though.&nbsp;

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:08.720
At the BlackRock Infrastructure Summit in March&nbsp;
2026, Sam Altman said something important.&nbsp;

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:41.760
Something massive is happening in AI,&nbsp;
not too dissimilar to other industries.&nbsp;

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:47.280
It turns out, Altman might be right,&nbsp;
but, maybe not in the way he hopes.&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:53.040
This is bad for all AI model&nbsp;
companies, not just OpenAI.&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.040
If you’ve watched this far, consider&nbsp;
subscribing! You probably enjoy our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:09.040
stuff, and it helps the channel too! Thanks.
Let’s give that earlier quote, a bit more context.&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.040
Altman is referencing a phrase made&nbsp;
by Lewis Strauss, Chair of the Atomic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:16.720
Energy Commission in the 50s, where he said
[Lewis Strauss]: “It is not too much to expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:21.520
that our children will enjoy in their homes&nbsp;
electrical energy too cheap to meter — will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.280
know of great periodic regional famines&nbsp;
in the world only as matters of history,—&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:30.640
will travel effort­lessly over the seas and&nbsp;
under them and through the air with a minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:36.640
of danger and at great speeds, — and will&nbsp;
experience a lifespan far longer than ours”&nbsp;

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:43.840
This was his prediction about nuclear power,&nbsp;
which never came true. Something else happened.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:48.640
Electricity became one of the most tightly&nbsp;
regulated industries in the developed world.&nbsp;

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:52.400
It still became a commodity,&nbsp;
with extremely thin margins.&nbsp;

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:55.840
A part of that ecosystem&nbsp;
absolutely makes a ton of money.&nbsp;

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:01.280
The turbine makers, transformer manufacturers,&nbsp;
and transmission networks are very profitable.&nbsp;

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:06.880
The value in electricity, moved down the&nbsp;
stack, or rather, it was always there anyway.&nbsp;

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:11.600
What makes all of this, so ironic, is that&nbsp;
Sam Altman literally predicted all of this.&nbsp;

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:15.040
In his June 2025 essay The Gentle Singularity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:18.320
he wrote that “as datacentre&nbsp;
production gets automated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:23.120
the cost of intelligence should eventually&nbsp;
converge to near the cost of electricity.”&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:42.240
It’s not just electricity either.
This clip must make it into the edit).&nbsp;

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:50.240
The internet eventually turned into a commodity.
A gigabit from Brand A is the same as Brand B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:54.000
so a customer has no reason to&nbsp;
consider anything but price.&nbsp;

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.840
The part that makes real money,&nbsp;
accumulates where it can’t move.&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:02.320
For a while, the companies selling the&nbsp;
actual equipment, like Cisco, and Corning.&nbsp;

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:06.240
Cisco was briefly the most valuable&nbsp;
company in the world in 2000.&nbsp;

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:10.960
But when the buildout of internet infrastructure&nbsp;
largely stopped, the value collapsed.&nbsp;

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:16.080
Now, the value is up the stack in the consumer&nbsp;
end. Companies like Google or Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:23.520
who have all your data, information, habits, have&nbsp;
a high switching cost, just not a monetary one.&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:29.040
So how does this relate to AI?
Because the exact same thing is happening.&nbsp;

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:09.840
In 2023, both GPUs and AI models were scarce.
But now, in 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:15.760
GPUs are still scarce, models are not.
And they’re all getting closer and closer.&nbsp;

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:21.280
“The gap between the best closed model and the&nbsp;
best open-weight model on Chatbot Arena fell from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:30.000
8.04% in January 2024 to 1.70% in February 2025.&nbsp;
The supply side is now effectively infinite. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:35.840
scarce asset becomes the ability to run them well&nbsp;
— throughput, latency, cost per token, routing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:41.840
caching, fine-tuning, evaluation, and guardrails.”
The value in all of these areas, accumulates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:46.800
where it is hardest to substitute.
The value, accumulating down the stack.&nbsp;

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:52.160
At the bottom, with the most “mass”, or&nbsp;
value, are the semiconductors and compute.&nbsp;

00:14:52.160 --> 00:15:00.640
“NVIDIA’s data-center revenue hit $75.2B, up&nbsp;
92% year over year, at a ~75% gross margin:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:06.560
roughly a $300B annualized run-rate”
The money is in companies selling GPUs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:12.160
semiconductors, compute. Not intelligence.
That might change, if the supply for GPUs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:15.840
and compute outweighs demand.
There is also value near the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:20.160
layer, not in ChatGPT, but in&nbsp;
third-party, consumer-facing software.&nbsp;

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:25.920
But this is rather thin, as AI apps have&nbsp;
lower margins than regular SaaS products.&nbsp;

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:30.320
Just look at Amazon.
They’re cutting back their AI model, Nova,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:35.520
because, at least I believe, they’ve figured out&nbsp;
this part of the industry isn't the money maker.&nbsp;

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:40.400
It’s the one below it.
This is why OpenAI is in such a tough spot.&nbsp;

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:45.200
They are stuck in the least valuable layer.
And as there are more comparable models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:50.000
and prices come down, paying those&nbsp;
massive bills might be tough.&nbsp;

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:56.480
What’s interesting is how AI and intelligence&nbsp;
have become a commodity, which is in tokens.&nbsp;

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:01.600
Charged by use, like electricity.
And now, token cost has even gone “far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:06.640
beyond the costs of the employees”.
But, something weird has happened.&nbsp;

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:10.960
Tokens aren’t actually getting more expensive.
The price of a single token has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:16.240
fallen by about 90% since 2023.
So, if intelligence and tokens are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:21.920
cheaper, why is AI spend getting so much bigger?
